2.2 Functions
Unlike Java where every methods must be declared inside a class, C++ allows functions to be declared
either inside or outside a class. When a function is declared inside a class declaration, it is called
a member function, otherwise it is a non-member or global function. All the functions shown in
Listing 1, are member functions. Any C++ program is required to deﬁne at least one global function,
the main() function.
